    "body": "## Security impact\n\nAttachment upload and delete previously locked a job by its global numeric id before checking whether the authenticated caller was a participant. For a private/non-public job, an authenticated outsider could therefore distinguish an existing foreign job from a nonexistent id through attachment mutation endpoints. Delete could additionally reach the attachment lookup before rejecting the caller.\n\n## Fix\n\n- add a participant-scoped pessimistic-write job lookup;\n- require that scoped lookup before attachment upload and delete;\n- return the existing neutral job-not-found response for non-participants;\n- stop outsider upload before attachment count/file validation/storage writes;\n- stop outsider delete before attachment, expense-claim, metadata, or storage access;\n- retain the existing attachment access policy after participant scoping, so legitimate participants still receive the correct role/status/ownership decision;\n- keep download/list visibility logic unchanged because those paths already have their own privacy boundary.\n\n## Financial/evidence invariants\n\nClaimed receipt immutability is unchanged. A receipt used for an expense claim is still blocked from deletion after participant authorization. Encryption/storage lifecycle and transaction-synchronized object cleanup are unchanged.\n\n## Tests\n\n- authorized upload still uses policy/file validation/storage and persists validated metadata;\n- outsider upload gets neutral not-found before policy/count/file/storage;\n- claimed receipt still cannot be deleted;\n- outsider delete gets neutral not-found before attachment/claim/storage access.\n\n## Schema\n\nNo Flyway migration.\n\n## Merge gate\n\nMerge only after all exact-head checks are green, `master` has not moved, branch is 0 behind, and the merged SHA is exactly the checked head SHA.",
